The energy density has three principal components in the model under discussion: radiation, nonrelativistic matter, and vacuum energy. Including curvature on the same bookkeeping line gives $\frac{C_r}{a^4}
+\frac{C_m}{a^3}
+\Lambda
-\frac{k}{a^2}
=H^2.$ Here $\Lambda$ uses the normalization of the preceding lecture, $\Lambda=(8\pi G/3)\rho_{\rm vac}$. Radiation dilutes as $a^{-4}$, matter as $a^{-3}$, vacuum energy does not dilute, and curvature scales as $a^{-2}$. Curvature is not a material energy density, but placing it beside the other terms makes the present constraint transparent: the four contributions must add to $H_0^2$.

